the iconic images of the Trans-Pecos to generations of travelers.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Capitan_(Texas)","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/El_Capitan_%284389347934%29.jpg/960px-El_Capitan_%284389347934%29.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_Capitan_(4389347934).jpg","dist_m":395,"width":640,"height":480,"license":"CC BY 2.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","artist":"Chris M Morris"}],"street":null},{"id":"node/356735775","name":"Guadalupe Peak","kind":"peak","lon":-104.86067,"lat":31.89146,"ele_m":2657.0,"wikidata":"Q32793","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guadalupe_Peak","tour":1,"summary":{"extract":"Guadalupe Peak, also known as Signal Peak, is the highest natural point in Texas, with an elevation of 8,751 feet (2,667 m) above sea level. It is located in Guadalupe Mountains National Park, and is part of the Guadalupe Mountains range in southeastern New Mexico and West Texas. The mountain is about 90 miles (140 km) east of El Paso and about 50 miles (80 km) southwest of Carlsbad, New Mexico. The peak rises more than 3,000 feet (910 m) above the arid floor of the Chihuahuan Desert.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guadalupe_Peak","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Guadalupe_Peak_National_Park.jpg/960px-Guadalupe_Peak_National_Park.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guadalupe_Peak_National_Park.jpg","dist_m":4,"width":640,"height":366,"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","artist":"Peter A. Episcopo"}],"street":null},{"id":"node/9184328746","name":"Pine Springs","kind":"place","lon":-104.81016,"lat":31.89118,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q7195357","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pine_Springs,_Texas","tour":3,"summary":{"extract":"Pine Springs is an unincorporated community in northern Culberson County, Texas, United States. According to the Handbook of Texas, the community had a population of 20 in 2000.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pine_Springs%2C_Texas","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[],"street":null}],"tour":["node/356730186","node/356735775","node/356733656","node/9184328746"],"tour_source":"auto","missing_stops":[]}